data.content:
Here are the recommended replacements:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)
    Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0); // or CollectionAssert.That is not empty:
    CollectionAssert.AreNotEqual(new List<User>(), users.ToList()); // option A

    // Better alternatives:
    Assert.AreNotEqual(0, users.Count);           // clearer failure message

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive))
    CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(users.ToList());        // for null checks
    // For predicate-based checks, MSTest doesn't have a built-in "All match predicate"
    // but you can use:
    foreach (var u in users)
        Assert.IsTrue(u.IsActive, $"User {u.Name} is not active");

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);              // gives "Expected:3 Actual:5" on failure

    // Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);
}
```

**Summary of key swaps:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.AreNotEqual(0, x.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count == n)` | `Assert.AreEqual(n, x.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(x))` | `CollectionAssert.Contains(list, x)` |

**Other useful `CollectionAssert` methods:**

- `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique(list)`
- `CollectionAssert.Contains(list, item)`
- `CollectionAssert.IsSubsetOf(subset, superset)`
- `CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expected, actual)` — same items, any order

The main benefit: when a test fails, these give you a meaningful message (e.g., *"Expected: 3, Actual: 5"*) instead of just *"Assert.IsTrue failed"*.
